WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.360
In 1982, a series of events in and around&nbsp;
Chicago transformed consumer safety.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.860
On the morning of September 29,&nbsp;
12-year-old Mary Kellerman told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.600
her parents she wasn’t feeling well, so they&nbsp;
gave her an extra-strength Tylenol capsule.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.480
Immediately after, Mary collapsed.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.400
She was dead by 10 am.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.020
That same day, postal worker&nbsp;
Adam Janus came down with a cold.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.500
He purchased some extra-strength&nbsp;
Tylenol at a supermarket.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.340
He took two capsules and&nbsp;
immediately went into convulsions.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.320
He later died in hospital.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.400
Doctors suspected the&nbsp;
27-year-old had a heart attack.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.440
After grieving at the hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.360
Adam’s family came together at his home to&nbsp;
comfort his wife and two small children.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.640
His brother Stanley was so grief-stricken&nbsp;
he came down with a headache. He grabbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.540
some Tylenol from the same bottle&nbsp;
Adam had purchased earlier that day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.760
He offered some to his newly wedded wife,&nbsp;
Theresa. They took two capsules each.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.280
Soon after, they collapsed.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.260
Stanley died that day. Theresa held on&nbsp;
for two more days before passing away.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.020
The unusual deaths caught the attention of&nbsp;
firefighters from the affected suburbs of Chicago.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.840
Firefighter Richard Keyworth said: “‘This&nbsp;
is a wild stab, but maybe it’s Tylenol.”

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.640
Another firefighter who had the same suspicions&nbsp;
reached out to a nurse from the local community.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.780
Nurse Helen Jensen spoke to Adam Janus’ widow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.140
realized all three family&nbsp;
members had taken Tylenol.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.720
She thought: “It has to be the Tylenol”.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.520
But she said the medical examiner and police&nbsp;
didn’t believe her and even laughed it off.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.920
In another suburb of Chicago, Mary&nbsp;
Reiner was feeling woozy. She had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.940
given birth to her fourth child two&nbsp;
days earlier. The hospital gave her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.380
some Tylenol to help with her postpartum&nbsp;
recovery, and she also purchased some from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:58.020
a store. Immediately after taking two&nbsp;
capsules, she felt dizzy and collapsed.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.580
Mary died the following day…at the same&nbsp;
hospital where she had just given birth.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.460
Another Mary, Mary McFarland, was taking a dinner&nbsp;
break in the shopping center where she worked.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:14.400
The 31-year-old single mom had a migraine. She&nbsp;
took two Tylenol capsules and, minutes later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.100
collapsed. She never regained consciousness.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.840
That evening, flight attendant&nbsp;
Paula Prince landed at Chicago’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.360
O’Hare International Airport after a long day.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.000
She stopped at a Walgreens on her way home.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.700
An ATM surveillance camera captured the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.580
35-year-old purchasing the&nbsp;
pills that would kill her.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.980
When she failed to show up for work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.960
her sister and a colleague went to her&nbsp;
apartment and found her lifeless body.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.680
All seven people died after taking Tylenol.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.660
When an investigator with the medical examiner’s&nbsp;
office first examined the Tylenol bottle from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.940
Mary Kellerman’s house and the other one from&nbsp;
the Janus’, they smelled like bitter almonds.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.940
The examiner knew instantly&nbsp;
what he was dealing with.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.980
The odor was cyanide.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.260
He was lucky to catch a whiff of&nbsp;
cyanide because only about 60% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.140
the population can detect the bitter almond scent.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:12.900
Out of the 44 remaining capsules in the Janus’&nbsp;
bottle, four were found to contain cyanide.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:18.120
Each capsule had between 550&nbsp;
to 610 milligrams of potassium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.900
cyanide - nearly three times the&nbsp;
amount needed to kill someone.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.980
Their blood samples tested&nbsp;
positive for cyanide poisoning.&nbsp;

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.600
A dogged reporter named John Flynn&nbsp;
Rooney received a tip and broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.260
the story of the poisonings before an&nbsp;
official press conference was called.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.740
Stores began pulling pain&nbsp;
relievers from the shelves.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.620
Chicago police drove through the streets using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.340
loudspeakers to order people to&nbsp;
flush Tylenol down the toilet. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:49.020
These quick actions likely saved more&nbsp;
lives as no further deaths occurred.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:58.620
The corporation that owned Tylenol, Johnson&nbsp;
&amp; Johnson, did NOT think this was the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.660
of an employee. It believed the capsules were&nbsp;
laced with cyanide after leaving the factories.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:07.440
Because the poisoned capsules came&nbsp;
from multiple manufacturing plants.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:13.140
Investigators believed someone must have taken the&nbsp;
bottles off store shelves, pulled the two casings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:18.600
apart, laced them with poison, and then reattached&nbsp;
the halves before returning them to the store.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.560
The capsule on the left is&nbsp;
regular Tylenol, the capsule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.720
on the right contains cyanide. The powder is&nbsp;
off-white and grayish in color, and lumpier.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.260
Store management pulled all the&nbsp;
Tylenol off the shelves on Thursday

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.937
The poisonings were the&nbsp;
biggest story in the country.

00:04:33.937 --> 00:04:38.700
Poison control center. We’ve been receiving&nbsp;
calls about once every 15 seconds.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.760
Tylenol was the most popular&nbsp;
pain reliever in America.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.940
Extra strength tylenol. Aspirin-free. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.640
you can’t buy a more potent pain&nbsp;
reliever without a prescription

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.120
Tylenol controlled 37% of the over-the-counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.440
pain reliever market and accounted for&nbsp;
$400 million in sales the year before.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.060
After the deaths, Tylenol’s&nbsp;
market share plummeted to 7%.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.220
Johnson &amp; Johnson recalled all 31&nbsp;
million bottles of Tylenol capsules.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:10.740
More tainted capsules were found. Fortunately,&nbsp;
they had not been sold or consumed.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.580
The poisonings changed how&nbsp;
over-the-counter drugs were packaged.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.260
At the time, there was no protective sealing&nbsp;
on any over-the-counter drug bottles.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:22.260
Johnson &amp; Johnson worked with the FDA to include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.220
safety seals which became the&nbsp;
norm for packaged food as well.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.800
The company also introduced a new version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.120
its pill - the caplet - which was harder&nbsp;
to tamper with than the older capsules.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.680
A few years later, Tylenol stopped&nbsp;
selling its capsule version for good.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.960
Johnson &amp; Johnson spent $100 million&nbsp;
on the recall and relaunch of Tylenol.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.220
And it was worth it.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.160
Within a year, Tylenol sales bounced back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.300
and once again became America’s&nbsp;
favorite over-the-counter pain reliever.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.500
But for the families of the victims,&nbsp;
there was no relief from their pain.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.600
Police had not caught the person responsible&nbsp;
for the deaths of their loved ones.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.400
But they did have someone in mind.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.200
About a week after the poisonings,&nbsp;
Tylenol’s manufacturer, McNeil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.920
a subsidiary of Johnson &amp; Johnson,&nbsp;
received a letter in the mail.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.040
Inside was a note written in all caps.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.860
Gentlemen: as you can see it is easy to&nbsp;
place cyanide (both potassium and sodium)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.142
into capsules sitting on store shelves. and&nbsp;
since the cyanide is inside the gelatin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.142 --> 00:06:22.270
it is easy to get buyers to swallow the bitter&nbsp;
pill. another beauty is that cyanide operates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.271 --> 00:06:22.388
quickly. it takes so very little, and there&nbsp;
will be no time to take countermeasures.

00:06:22.388 --> 00:06:22.505
If you don’t mind the publicity of these&nbsp;
little capsules, then do nothing. so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.505 --> 00:06:22.620
i’ve spent less than 50 dollars and it&nbsp;
takes me less than 10 minutes per bottle.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:26.820
If you want to stop the killing&nbsp;
then wire $1,000,000 to bank account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:32.760
number #84-49-597 at continental&nbsp;
illinois bank, Chicago, Illinois.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:35.400
Was this really the killer?

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:40.680
The FBI discovered that the bank account&nbsp;
belonged to Frederick Miller McCahey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.140
the heir to the Miller Brewing fortune.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.780
However, police didn’t&nbsp;
believe he wrote the letter.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:51.180
The bank account mentioned in the note had been&nbsp;
closed after McCahey shut down his travel agency.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.080
The letter was traced to his&nbsp;
agency’s Pitney-Bowes stamp machine.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:59.880
Was the letter writer someone who had&nbsp;
access to the agency and held a grudge?

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.820
Police learned that one of&nbsp;
McCahey’s former employees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:08.520
Nancy Richardson, was upset that her&nbsp;
final paycheck of $511 had bounced.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:13.560
Nancy’s husband, Robert Richardson, had&nbsp;
angrily confronted McCahey over the check.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:18.180
The Richardsons had moved out of Chicago&nbsp;
just weeks before the poisonings.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.460
When police issued a warrant&nbsp;
for the couple’s arrest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:23.280
they discovered they had been using fake names.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.640
Robert and Nancy Richardson were aliases.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.700
Their real names were: James and LeAnn Lewis.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:34.200
Authorities tracked them down in&nbsp;
New York City and arrested James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:39.120
Lewis at the New York Public Library.
Lewis’ fingerprints matched those on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.280
the extortion letter sent to Johnson &amp; Johnson.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.460
He said he never intended&nbsp;
to collect the cash and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.860
simply trying to embarrass his wife’s former boss.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.360
Lewis may have been the letter&nbsp;
writer, but was he the Tylenol killer?

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:56.550
During his sentencing hearing in the&nbsp;
extortion case, he sat down with CBS 2.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.040
Did you commit the Tylenol murders?
I did not.&nbsp;

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:04.500
Do you know who did?
I do not know who did, no.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.960
But investigators were not convinced.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:07.440
Clip A new development in the Tylenol murder case.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.680
Some believe Lewis resembled&nbsp;
the man caught on surveillance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:14.880
video watching flight attendant Paula&nbsp;
Prince purchase the Tylenol capsules.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.320
Investigators were fortunate to get&nbsp;
this shot because the ATM security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.160
camera only captured still photos&nbsp;
when someone was using the machine.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:26.760
As investigators dug into James Lewis’ background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:30.840
they learned he had a troubled&nbsp;
past and faced a murder charge.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.740
When he was a little boy,&nbsp;
his father abandoned him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:39.960
then his mother. A couple adopted him at the&nbsp;
age of two and raised him on a farm in Missouri.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:45.840
As a teen, he attacked his adoptive mother with&nbsp;
an axe and spent time in a mental institution.

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.440
He attended the University&nbsp;
of Missouri-Kansas City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.380
where he met LeAnn. They&nbsp;
married and had a daughter.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.120
Toni Ann was born with Down’s syndrome&nbsp;
and had other health problems.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.640
By all accounts, Lewis doted on her.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:05.220
An FBI report stated: “He never showed anger&nbsp;
about Toni’s condition or blamed anyone.”

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:10.380
The couple opened a tax accounting business&nbsp;
in Kansas City called Lewis &amp; Lewis.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:14.160
One of their clients was an&nbsp;
elderly man named Raymond West.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.480
West lived a simple, retired life&nbsp;
after working as a truck driver.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.820
He kept the rose bushes in front of his home&nbsp;
perfectly pruned and took walks every day.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.040
But one day, he…disappeared.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:30.120
When a friend went to his home to&nbsp;
check on him, he found a note taped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:35.820
to the front door which read: “Ray is out of&nbsp;
town until Thursday. For further, call Jim”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.220
It was written on Lewis &amp; Lewis stationery.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.520
When West’s friend returned to&nbsp;
the house a few weeks later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.080
he smelled a foul odor emanating&nbsp;
from a bedroom and called police.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.580
When police arrived, they looked&nbsp;
in the attic, and to their horror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.920
found Raymond West’s decomposing&nbsp;
body. His legs had been severed.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:59.460
Police quickly zoned in on a suspect: James Lewis.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:04.620
West had made out a cheque to Lewis&nbsp;
for $5,000 dated the last day anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:09.300
had heard from him. A handwriting expert&nbsp;
concluded the check was likely forged.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:13.500
When police searched Lewis’ cars,&nbsp;
they found dozens of canceled checks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.240
belonging to West. They also found a rope&nbsp;
matching the rope found around West’s body.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.840
Lewis denied killing West&nbsp;
and said the rope was from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.140
the time he helped West rebuild his&nbsp;
home after the flood a year before.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:29.520
There was plenty of circumstantial&nbsp;
evidence linking Lewis to the crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.680
and prosecutors charged him with murder.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.940
However, the case was dismissed&nbsp;
due to a technicality.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:40.980
Lewis should have been read his Miranda rights&nbsp;
before questioning when he was arrested and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:45.480
brought to the police station. This is the&nbsp;
“You have the right to remain silent” warning.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.000
But police never read him his rights.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.900
A judge ruled that police acted&nbsp;
improperly in arresting Lewis…and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:57.120
threw out the evidence taken from Lewis’ cars&nbsp;
and all the statements he made to police.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:01.380
Prosecutors lost some of their&nbsp;
best circumstantial evidence.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.020
There was one other piece of evidence.

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:09.060
A fingerprint was found on the pulley&nbsp;
used to hoist West’s body into the attic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.000
but police had not been able&nbsp;
to match the print to Lewis.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:15.900
Three years later, the FBI used state-of-the-art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:18.780
equipment to confirm that the&nbsp;
fingerprint belonged to Lewis.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:22.140
However, Lewis was never re-charged with murder.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.060
A reason someone is not charged despite&nbsp;
new evidence is that it might not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.360
sufficient to meet the burden of&nbsp;
proof required for a conviction.

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:34.260
What about the Tylenol murders -&nbsp;
could Lewis have been behind those?

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.960
Investigators believed he had a motive.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:38.880
And it had to do with his daughter.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:44.220
Lewis’ daughter Toni Ann had a heart&nbsp;
defect that required multiple surgeries.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:50.160
When she was five, the sutures used to repair&nbsp;
the hole in her heart tore apart, and she died.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:55.980
A federal investigation later confirmed that&nbsp;
the polypropylene sutures that tore were sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:01.020
under the brand name Prolene, according to&nbsp;
records reviewed by the Chicago Tribune.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:06.360
Prolene was produced by….Johnson &amp;&nbsp;
Johnson, the company that made Tylenol.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:10.740
Lewis blamed the drug manufacturer&nbsp;
for the death of his little girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:14.220
according to a police sergeant&nbsp;
quoted in a Memphis newspaper.&nbsp;

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:19.560
He served 13 years for extortion for writing&nbsp;
the ransom letter to Johnson &amp; Johnson.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:22.620
While in prison, he produced elaborate sketches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:25.860
that showed how cyanide powder&nbsp;
could be packed into capsules.

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:31.920
But he insisted he was innocent and was simply&nbsp;
trying to help investigators solve the crime.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:36.660
He explained to the Chicago Tribune, “I&nbsp;
could tell you how Julius Caesar was killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:39.420
but that does not mean I was the killer.”

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:44.940
Lewis was not the only suspect. Police&nbsp;
investigated around 400 people, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:49.440
Roger Arnold, who worked in the warehouse of&nbsp;
one of the stores that sold the tainted Tylenol.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:54.660
Arnold also worked with the father of one of the&nbsp;
victims, Mary Reiner, who had just given birth.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:59.940
The owner of a bar that Arnold frequented&nbsp;
grew concerned after a couple of regulars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:04.620
reported that Arnold had been talking about&nbsp;
possessing cyanide and working on a project.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:07.800
The bar owner subsequently tipped off police.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:12.900
When Arnold found out, he became&nbsp;
furious and ended up shooting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:15.960
man he mistakenly believed was the bar owner.

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:18.900
The man he shot, a father of three, died.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:22.620
And Arnold was sentenced to 30 years in prison.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:26.520
But there was still no one&nbsp;
behind bars for the poisonings.

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:32.340
A few weeks after the murders, the Chicago&nbsp;
Tribune appealed to the killer to come forward.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:35.520
Journalist Bob Greene focused on the suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:39.360
of the parents of young Mary&nbsp;
Kellerman. His article read:

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:43.620
If you are the Tylenol killer,&nbsp;
some of this may matter to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.020
Or it may make no difference at all.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:49.140
If you are the Tylenol killer, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:54.060
you may be harboring just the vaguest curiosity&nbsp;
about the people on the other end of your plan:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:58.698
the people who were unfortunate enough&nbsp;
to purchase the bottles you had touched.

00:13:58.698 --> 00:13:58.752
If you are curious, come to&nbsp;
